\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Parity and adjoint on the four-cycle}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring. On the
[four-cycle](fdip-0003), take #{V=\mathbb Z/4\mathbb Z},
#{H=\mathcal F_R(V)} and the additional partition
#{V_0=\{0,2\}}, #{V_1=\{1,3\}}. The
\vocabk{cyclic shift}{fdip-0009} #{T:H\to H} is
#{(Tf)(i)=f(i+1)}. It exchanges the two support submodules:
if #{f} is supported on #{V_j}, then #{Tf} is supported on
#{V_{j+1}}. Hence #{T} is odd in the
[chosen operator grading](fdip-001K).}

\p{For the \vocabk{counting adjoint}{fdip-0007}
constructed over #{R} in \ref{fdip-001H},
reindexing the finite sum proves
##{b(Tf,g)=\sum_i f(i+1)g(i)
 =\sum_j f(j)g(j-1)=b(f,T^{-1}g),}
so #{T^\dagger=T^{-1}}. Also #{T^4=I}; thus the half-turn
#{T^2} is even and satisfies
#{(T^2)^\dagger=T^{-2}=T^2}. These unweighted counting
identities do not assert that the one-step shift preserves the
alternating weights. The weight-preserving half-turn and the
one-step counterexample remain those in \ref{fdip-0003}.}

\tikzfigs{0.55}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  field/.style={draw,rounded corners,minimum width=12mm,
    minimum height=9mm,inner sep=2mm},
  shiftedge/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[field] (d0) at (0,2.4) {$\delta_0$};
\node[field] (d1) at (3.2,2.4) {$\delta_1$};
\node[field] (d2) at (3.2,0) {$\delta_2$};
\node[field] (d3) at (0,0) {$\delta_3$};
\draw[shiftedge] (d0) -- node[left=3mm] {$T$} (d3);
\draw[shiftedge] (d3) -- node[below=3mm] {$T$} (d2);
\draw[shiftedge] (d2) -- node[right=3mm] {$T$} (d1);
\draw[shiftedge] (d1) -- node[above=3mm] {$T$} (d0);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The arrows show the action on basis fields, opposite to the
source-to-head orientation on vertices. Every arrow switches support
parity; two steps give the half-turn.
In particular, over #{\mathbb Z},
#{T^2\delta_0=\delta_2\ne0}. Thus an odd operator need not
square to zero. It need not satisfy a graded commutation law
either: that law applied to #{T,T} would give #{2T^2=0},
whereas #{2T^2\delta_0=2\delta_2\ne0}.}

\p{Identify edge #{i} with vertex #{i}, with source #{i} and
head #{i+1}, as in \ref{fdip-0009}. Only after this identification
does incidence become the endomorphism #{\Delta=T-I}.
Its even component is #{-I} and its odd component is #{T}.
Over #{\mathbb Z}, both are nonzero, so #{\Delta} is
inhomogeneous, and
##{\Delta^2\delta_0=\delta_2-2\delta_3+\delta_0\ne0.}
The formulas remain valid over every commutative unital ring,
but the nonzero witnesses just used are specifically over
#{\mathbb Z}; in the zero ring all these fields and maps vanish.
This is a direct specialization of the
[shift-multiplication realization](fdip-0013) and
[partition construction](fdip-001I), not a square-zero
differential supplied by parity.}

\p{\citet{Section 1, accepted-manuscript pp. 1-2}{wieser2022graded} gives the grading convention;
the shift, adjoint and failure witnesses are the computations here.}
